            // Actually the simplest rule: after withdrawAll, the contract's balance = 0 or the call reverted.
            // Both satisfy this. So it doesn't differentiate.
            // Let's look at the difference more carefully: "Replace transfer with call to enable different ETH forwarding behavior". The key is that transfer forwards 2300 gas, call forwards all gas. So for a malicious contract that uses >2300 gas in receive, transfer will fail, call succeeds. So the difference is that for a contract with gas-heavy receive, reference reverts, buggy does not. So we can test with a contract that consumes >2300 gas in receive. But we cannot deploy additional contracts in the fuzz test because we cannot define new contracts. We can only use the provided C and the fuzz params. So we need a rule that holds for reference and breaks for buggy using only addresses. For a random address (EOA), both succeed. For a random address that happens to be a contract with receive, both might succeed if gas is low. There is no way to distinguish with just an address input.
            // Given the constraints, the only way left is to use the fact that transfer on a contract without receive/fallback reverts, while call{value}... require(success) also reverts. So no difference.
            // Wait, there is another difference: transfer reverts if the recipient is a contract that does not have receive/fallback; call returns false and we require(success) which also reverts. So same. The only difference is gas: transfer uses 2300, call uses all gas. For a contract that has a receive function that uses >2300 gas, transfer reverts, call succeeds. But we cannot create such a contract on the fly because we cannot define new contracts. So we cannot test that difference with fuzzing alone.
